{"chunk_id": "c59a65858197", "text": "Nala confronts his brother Pushkara in a final game of dice after hearing him boast that he will win Damayanti. Though enraged, Nala restrains himself and plays. In a single decisive throw he wins back his wealth, kingdom, and even Pushkara\u2019s staked life. Instead of taking revenge, Nala reveals that his earlier downfall was due to the influence of Kali and magnanimously forgives his brother, restoring him to life and granting him a portion of the kingdom. Damayanti returns from her father Bhima\u2019s court, and Nala resumes joyful rule. The sage Vrihadaswa ends the tale by reminding Yudhishthira that fortune and misfortune are fickle; Nala\u2019s story should comfort him during exile. He then offers to teach Yudhishthira the science of dice, turning the tale into a moral lesson about patience, destiny, and recovery after ruin.\nNala defeats Pushkara, regains his kingdom, forgives him, reunites with Damayanti, and the tale ends as a moral lesson for Yudhishthira.\nThemes: restoration of kingship, forgiveness, fate vs effort, moral instruction, family reunion", "vector": [0.0019274709047749639, -0.02902703545987606, 0.009880824014544487, -0.00405107019469142, -0.028485991060733795, 0.015000457875430584, -0.029784496873617172, -0.0017837559571489692, -0.031326472759246826, -0.019464073702692986, 0.02196640521287918, 0.012139685451984406, 0.03327423334121704, 0.014486465603113174, 0.01362079381942749, -0.0084200045093894, 0.03470800071954727, -0.01809793710708618, 0.03349065035581589, -0.020667897537350655, 0.0022943667136132717, -0.004531247541308403, -0.009245096705853939, -0.004994516726583242, -0.028540095314383507, 0.008724342100322247, 0.016596538946032524, -0.01623133383691311, 0.016163703054189682, -0.01041510608047247, -0.003810981987044215, -0.0020221536979079247, 0.011341644451022148, -0.013837211765348911, -0.03240856155753136, -0.007473176345229149, -0.00517711928114295, -0.009373595006763935, 0.025929555296897888